EXPERIMENT 26 – BOUNDARY VALUE ANALYSIS USING JUNIT
AIM
To write JUnit test cases to implement Boundary Value Analysis for a student grade evaluation system using JUnit.
ALGORITHM
1. Create a Maven project and add the JUnit 5 junit-jupiter dependency to pom.xml.
2. Create the GradeEvaluator class with the grade(int marks) method.
3. Define grade ranges:
   - A: 90–100
   - B: 80–89
   - C: 70–79
   - D: 60–69
   - E: 50–59
   - F: 0–49
4. Identify the lower boundary, upper boundary and values just outside each range.
5. Write a @ParameterizedTest with @CsvSource for the 12 valid boundary values.
6. Write another @ParameterizedTest with @ValueSource(ints = {-1, 101}) to test invalid marks using IllegalArgumentException.
7. Run tests using mvn test and verify the output.
CODE
GradeEvaluator.java
public class GradeEvaluator {
    public static String grade(int marks) {
        if (marks < 0 || marks > 100) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                "Marks must be between 0 and 100");
        }
        if (marks >= 90) return "A";
        if (marks >= 80) return "B";
        if (marks >= 70) return "C";
        if (marks >= 60) return "D";
        if (marks >= 50) return "E";
        return "F";
    }
}


GradeEvaluatorBVATest.java
import org.junit.jupiter.params.ParameterizedTest;
import org.junit.jupiter.params.provider.CsvSource;
import org.junit.jupiter.params.provider.ValueSource;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

public class GradeEvaluatorBVATest {

    @ParameterizedTest(name = "marks {0} -> grade {1}")
    @CsvSource({
        "0,F", "49,F", "50,E", "59,E",
        "60,D", "69,D", "70,C", "79,C",
        "80,B", "89,B", "90,A", "100,A"
    })
    void validBoundaryValues(int marks, String expected) {
        assertEquals(expected, GradeEvaluator.grade(marks));
    }

    @ParameterizedTest(name = "invalid marks {0}")
    @ValueSource(ints = {-1, 101})
    void invalidBoundaryValues(int marks) {
        assertThrows(IllegalArgumentException.class,
            () -> GradeEvaluator.grade(marks));
    }
}


